<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the difference between cellulitis and erysipelas?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">keyboard_arrow_down</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Both are bacterial skin infections, but erysipelas affects the upper dermal layers (epidermis and upper dermis) while cellulitis affects the deeper subcutaneous fat. Erysipelas produces a raised, sharply demarcated bright red patch with a clear border — often called "cellulitis with a border." Erysipelas responds rapidly to penicillin antibiotics. Both require the same antibiotic approach for the face.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How quickly does facial cellulitis respond to antibiotics?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">keyboard_arrow_down</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Most patients show measurable improvement within 24–48 hours of starting appropriate antibiotics — reduced redness, warmth, and swelling. If there is no improvement within 48–72 hours, the diagnosis should be reconsidered (abscess formation, resistant bacteria, or wrong antibiotic). Call us on +91 90006 66476 if facial redness is worsening despite antibiotics.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can facial cellulitis be prevented?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">keyboard_arrow_down</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Facial cellulitis from dental sources can be prevented by treating dental infections promptly — not delaying root canal treatment or tooth extractions. Skin-origin cellulitis can be reduced by proper wound care of facial cuts, avoiding scratching insect bites, and treating skin conditions that cause breaks in the skin. Good oral hygiene reduces dental-origin facial cellulitis risk.</div>
</details>
